We refer to the vertex of maximum height in this tree as the \textit{root}, and denote the term tree of a term $r$ by $\T_r$. 

\begin{claim}\label{elim}
If $d_1,\dots, d_n \in (A \cup \{0\})^m$, $e \in A^m$, and $e \in \langle d_1, \dots, d_n \rangle_{\A_\M^m}$, then there exists a term $p(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ in the language $\F$ such that
\[
p^{\A_\M^m}(d_1,\dots,d_n) =e.
\]
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{$\blacksquare$}
Let $d_1,\dots, d_n \in (A \cup \{0\})^m$ and $e \in A^m$. Let $p(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ be a term in the language $\F \cup \H$ such that $p^{\A_\M^m}(d_1,\dots,d_n)=e$. We assume $p(x_1,\dots, x_n)$ was chosen so that $\T_p$ has the minimum number of vertices with labels from $\H$ and satisfies $p^{\A_\M^m}(d_1,\dots, d_n)=e$.  If $\T_p$ has no label from $\H$, then the claim is proven. So we assume $\T_p$ has at least one vertex with label from $\H$. We will analyze the term $p(x_1,\dots, x_n)$ in parallel with the evaluation of $p$ at $d_1,\dots,d_n$, as illustrated in \cref{fig2}.

Choose a maximal vertex with respect to height with label from $\H$, and say the label is $h \in \H$. Call this vertex $\nu$. The subtree of $\T_p$ whose root is $\nu$ corresponds to a subterm $q$ of $p$. If $h$ is $k$-ary, the vertex $\nu$ has $k$ edges corresponding to $k$ subterms of $q$, which we will denote as $s_1,\dots, s_k$. For $1\leq i \leq k$, we define $c_i:=s^{\A_\M^m}_i(d_1,\dots,d_n)$. Set $z :=h^{\A_\M^m}(c_1,\dots, c_k)$. For $1 \leq j \leq m$, we write $z|_j$ to denote the $j^{th}$ coordinate of the $m$-tuple $z$. 
\begin{figure}[hbt]
	\begin{tikzpicture}
	\node at (0,2.5) {$\T_p$};
	\draw[] (0,2) circle (.2cm);
	\draw (-.15,1.9) -- (-2.5,-2.5);
	\draw (.15,1.9) -- (2.5,-2.5);

	\node at (0,.4) {$\T_q$};
	\draw (0,0) circle (.2cm);
	\node at (0,0) {$h$};
	\draw (0,-.2) -- (-.35,-.35);
	\draw (0,-.2) -- (.35,-.35);
	\draw[] (-.5,-.5) circle (.2cm);
	\draw[] (.5,-.5) circle (.2cm);
	\node at (-.7,-.1) {$\T_{s_1}$};
	\node at (.7,-.1) {$\T_{s_k}$};
	\draw[dotted] (-.3,-.5) -- (.3,-.5);
	\draw (-.5,-.7) -- (-1.5,-2.5);
	\draw (.5,-.7) -- (1.5,-2.5);
